Candidate, 2026-11-20: the owner's --idle-phase option (experiment, off by default).
Not applied in the tree: dante/owner/main.cpp stays the source of the installed owner
bc486911 (8c9bf80e, pinned in install/dev-unit/owner-monitor.sha256). Apply to a copy:

    patch -p1 < dante/owner/idle-phase.patch        (from the repository's root)
    g++ -std=c++20 -O2 -Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic -Werror -I dante/owner -I dsp-core/include \
        -o usb16x16-dante-owner-idlephase dante/owner/main.cpp dsp-core/src/bridge_core.cpp -lasound -lpthread

What it changes: with --idle-phase US the idle grid ticks US microseconds after the phase the
host's last block arrived on. Without it (the default, and the installed behaviour, byte for
byte the same decisions) the grid starts one tick after the last timed-out wait, which began
when that wake's work ended, so a preset's stage time is in the grid's phase: about 50 us at
96 kHz, which finds the next session's prime 5 to 6 frames nearer the lead and, at 252 of 256,
a block late on some opens (PROJECT_HANDOFF.md, MEASURED and FOUND 2026-11-20). The START line
gains idle_phase_us=. Nothing else in the audio path is touched.

The value to try first is 40: the host starts a session on any 125 us microframe, the tick's
own work is about 40 us, so a tick 40 us after one microframe boundary ends 45 us before the
next. Check: at 96 kHz with a preset, opens repeated; tx_prime_found should sit where direct's
does today (about 164 and 248 with 40) and no open should read a block late.

